Here's a recommended structure:

**1. Make HTTP calls testable via dependency injection:**

```csharp
// In your MCP server project
public class WeatherTool
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;

    public WeatherTool(HttpClient httpClient)
    {
        _httpClient = httpClient;
    }

    public async Task<string> GetForecastAsync(string city)
    {
        var response = await _httpClient.GetAsync($"https://api.example.com/weather?city={city}");
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
    }
}
```

**2. Create the test project:**

```bash
dotnet new xunit -n MyMcpServer.Tests
cd MyMcpServer.Tests
dotnet add reference ../MyMcpServer/MyMcpServer.csproj
dotnet add package Moq           # or NSubstitute
dotnet add package RichardSzalay.MockHttp   # mock HttpClient easily
```

**3. Write tests with a mock HTTP handler:**

```csharp
using RichardSzalay.MockHttp;

public class WeatherToolTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task GetForecast_ReturnsExpectedData()
    {
        // Arrange — fake the external API
        var mockHttp = new MockHttpMessageHandler();
        mockHttp
            .When("https://api.example.com/weather?city=Seattle")
            .Respond("application/json", """{"temp": 72}""");

        var httpClient = mockHttp.ToHttpClient();
        var tool = new WeatherTool(httpClient);

        // Act
        var result = await tool.GetForecastAsync("Seattle");

        // Assert
        Assert.Contains("72", result);
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task GetForecast_HandlesApiError()
    {
        var mockHttp = new MockHttpMessageHandler();
        mockHttp
            .When("*")
            .Respond(System.Net.HttpStatusCode.ServiceUnavailable);

        var tool = new WeatherTool(mockHttp.ToHttpClient());

        await Assert.ThrowsAsync<HttpRequestException>(
            () => tool.GetForecastAsync("Seattle"));
    }
}
```

**Key principles:**

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **Inject `HttpClient`** | Pass it via constructor so tests can substitute it |
| **Mock at the handler level** | Use `MockHttpMessageHandler` — avoids wrapping `HttpClient` in a custom interface |
| **Test error paths** | Return 4xx/5xx from the mock to verify error handling |
| **If you wrap APIs in a service** | Extract an `IWeatherService` interface and mock that with Moq/NSubstitute instead |

**Project layout:**

```
src/
  MyMcpServer/
    Tools/
      WeatherTool.cs
    Services/
      IWeatherService.cs      # optional abstraction
      WeatherService.cs
tests/
  MyMcpServer.Tests/
    Tools/
      WeatherToolTests.cs
    Fixtures/
      SampleResponses/        # store JSON fixtures here
        weather-seattle.json
```

The `IHttpClientFactory` pattern (via `AddHttpClient<T>()` in DI) is another good option if your server already uses `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` — it integrates naturally with `MockHttpMessageHandler` in tests.
